WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.619
Tool execution controls are where MCP security becomes operational.

2
00:00:04.619 --> 00:00:08.847
Read-only tools and write-capable tools carry different risk.

3
00:00:08.847 --> 00:00:23.487
A read-only inventory query may expose sensitive data if poorly controlled, but a write-capable tool can modify files, send messages, create tickets, change cloud resources, update repositories, or trigger workflows.

4
00:00:23.487 --> 00:00:28.733
High-impact actions should never rely on the model alone to decide what is safe.

5
00:00:28.733 --> 00:00:39.928
Command execution, file modification, email, messaging, code repository changes, cloud actions, ticketing, and workflow automation all need external enforcement.

6
00:00:39.928 --> 00:00:47.131
The model can produce arguments or recommend an action, but validation should happen in deterministic code.

7
00:00:47.131 --> 00:00:49.871
Schema validation checks shape and type.

8
00:00:49.871 --> 00:00:53.943
Argument validation checks allowed values and constraints.

9
00:00:53.943 --> 00:00:57.309
Allowlisted operations limit what the tool can do.

10
00:00:57.309 --> 00:01:02.946
Policy checks decide whether this user, in this context, can request this operation.

11
00:01:02.946 --> 00:01:09.288
Transaction previews and dry-run modes help users understand consequences before execution.

12
00:01:09.288 --> 00:01:20.875
A preview can show what file will change, which ticket will be updated, who will receive a message, what cloud resource will be modified, or which repository branch is affected.

13
00:01:20.875 --> 00:01:28.077
Human approval gates are especially important for irreversible, costly, external, or privileged actions.

14
00:01:28.077 --> 00:01:35.437
Approval should be tied to the specific action and arguments, not just to a generic permission granted earlier.

15
00:01:35.437 --> 00:01:38.647
Rollback planning completes the control story.

16
00:01:38.647 --> 00:01:43.814
If a tool can make changes, the team should know how to undo or contain mistakes.

17
00:01:43.814 --> 00:01:52.504
That may involve version history, reversible workflows, backups, change records, rate limits, or incident response procedures.

18
00:01:52.504 --> 00:01:58.924
Safe operations assume that models can misunderstand context and users can approve too quickly.

19
00:01:58.924 --> 00:02:04.404
The system should make the safe path easy and enforce hard limits outside the model.
